WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.377
The oldest canyon rocks record metamorphism and granite emplacement around one

00:00:05.377 --> 00:00:06.910
point seven billion years ago.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.250
Uplift and erosion then exposed them.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:14.050
Late Precambrian sedimentary and volcanic rocks accumulated on that erosion surface

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.330
around one point one billion years ago.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:20.870
The lower unconformity records roughly six hundred million years absent between

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:21.550
these packages.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:27.470
Late Precambrian beds were tilted and eroded before Paleozoic sediment covered

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:31.790
them. This upper angular unconformity can cut across the older lower

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:35.930
unconformity. Where the surfaces merge, still more time is missing.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.650
Over granite, a sedimentary contact can be a nonconformity;

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.090
identify the actual rocks on both sides.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:47.570
Paleozoic sandstone, shale and limestone record changing marine and land environments.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.230
Their fossils constrain age, but disconformities interrupt the sequence.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.630
The canyon lacks entire periods and does not preserve every moment

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.930
within the periods present. A thick and colorful wall can still

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.750
represent less time than its gaps.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.450
Broad monoclines and faults affected the region after deposition.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.830
The supplied history places monocline formation between eighty and forty million

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.030
years ago, while much modern canyon cutting occurred within the last

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.050
five million years. Basalt on an older plateau and lava inside

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:19.270
an existing canyon constrain different stages of landscape development.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:23.670
The Colorado River exposes old rock without making the canyon equally

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:28.250
old. Faults that cut young lava show continued activity, and canyon-filling

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.510
lava shows that a canyon already existed.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.770
Combine these relations with isotope dates and fossils.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.330
State what is preserved, what is missing and which event each

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:38.850
age actually constrains.
